{"page": 26, "text": "Interest and Other Financial Income Interest and Other Financial Income is comprised of interest income on cash and marketable securities and other financial gains. The decrease of $15 million for the first nine months of 2026 compared to the first nine months of 2025, was primarily driven by a $49 million decrease in interest income on marketable securities due to the liquidation of all marketable securities to contribute to the funding of the Merus acquisition completed in December 2025. This decrease was partially offset by an increase of $23 million due to a modification gain related to the Term B Loans Amended Credit Agreement entered into in June 2026 and other financial gains related to the term loans. Refer to Financial Statement Note 8 in this interim report for further details about borrowings. Interest and Amortization Expense on Borrowings The increase of $324 million for the first nine months of 2026 compared to the first nine months of 2025, was due to $256 million of interest expense and $68 million of amortization of fees associated with the debt issued in December 2025 in connection with the financing of the Merus acquisition as well as the Term B Loans Amended Credit Agreement in June 2026. There was no interest expense or amortization of fees on borrowings for the first nine months of 2025 as Genmab did not have any borrowings prior to December 2025. Refer to Financial Statement Note 8 in this interim report for further details about borrowings. Gain on Marketable Securities, Net\nGain on marketable securities, net, which includes the impact of foreign exchange rate movements, decreased $63 million in the first nine months of 2026 compared to the first nine months of 2025. There were no gains or losses on marketable securities for the first nine months of 2026 due to the liquidation of all marketable securities to contribute to the funding of the Merus acquisition in December 2025.\nGenmab A/S\nCarl Jacobsens Vej 30\n2500 Valby, Denmark Tel: +45 7020 2728 www.genmab.com Company Announcement no. 45 Page 21/45 CVR no. 2102 3884\nInterim Report for the Nine Months of 2026 Foreign Exchange Rate Loss/Gain, Net Foreign exchange rate loss, net, was $10 million in the first nine months of 2026 compared to foreign exchange rate gain, net of $7 million in the first nine months of 2025. The decrease was primarily driven by foreign exchange rate movements impacting Genmab\u2019s EUR and DKK denominated assets and liabilities. The EUR and DKK weakened against the USD in the first nine months of 2026 compared to strengthening against the USD in the first nine months of 2025. Key Developments to Net Financial Items \u2013 Third Quarter of 2026\n"}
{"page": 27, "text": "Interest and Other Financial Income Interest and Other Financial Income is comprised of interest income on cash and marketable securities and other financial gains. The decrease of $7 million for the third quarter of 2026 compared to the third quarter of 2025, was primarily driven by the decrease in interest income on marketable securities due to the liquidation of all marketable securities to contribute to the funding of the Merus acquisition completed in December 2025. Refer to Financial Statement Note 8 in this interim report for further details about borrowings. Interest and Amortization Expense on Borrowings The increase of $107 million for the third quarter of 2026 compared to the third quarter of 2025, was due to $82 million of interest expense and $25 million of amortization of fees associated with the debt issued in December 2025 in connection with the financing of the Merus acquisition as well as the Term B Loans Amended Credit Agreement in June 2026. There was no interest expense or amortization of fees on borrowings for the third quarter of 2025 as Genmab did not have any borrowings prior to December 2025. Refer to Financial Statement Note 8 in this interim report for further details about borrowings. Gain on Marketable Securities, Net Gain on marketable securities, net, which includes the impact of foreign exchange rate movements, decreased $6 million in the third quarter of 2026 compared to the third quarter of 2025. There were no gains or losses on marketable securities for the third quarter of 2026 due to the liquidation of all marketable securities to contribute to the funding of the Merus acquisition in December 2025. Foreign Exchange Rate Loss/Gain, Net Foreign exchange rate gain, net, was $4 million in the third quarter of 2026 compared to foreign exchange rate loss, net of $10 million in the third quarter of 2025. The increase was primarily driven by reduced foreign currency exposure on Genmab\u2019s foreign denominated assets and liabilities in the third quarter of 2026 compared to the third quarter of 2025, partly offset by negative foreign exchange rate movements impacting Genmab\u2019s foreign denominated assets and liabilities. The third quarter result included reductions of $13 million in foreign exchange gains and $17 million in foreign exchange losses, presented within financial income and financial expenses, respectively, consistent with their year-to-date classification. Corporate Tax\n"}
{"page": 28, "text": "Corporate tax expense for the first nine months of 2026 was $25 million compared to $217 million for the first nine months of 2025. The decrease in corporate tax expense is the result of Genmab\u2019s lower net profit before tax and decrease in the estimated annual effective tax rate in the first nine months ended 2026 to 3.4%, compared to 18.9% in the first nine months of 2025. The lower estimated annual effective tax rate was primarily attributable to the Merus integration that allows for the partial recognition of previously unrecognized tax benefits. Accordingly, the estimated annual effective tax rate for the first nine months ended 2026 is not indicative of the Company's expected annual effective tax rate in future periods. The annual effective tax rate may experience further volatility from the statutory rate as Merus integration continues.\nGenmab A/S\nCarl Jacobsens Vej 30\n2500 Valby, Denmark Tel: +45 7020 2728 www.genmab.com Company Announcement no. 45 Page 22/45 CVR no. 2102 3884\nInterim Report for the Nine Months of 2026 Key Developments to Corporate Tax \u2013 Third Quarter of 2026 Corporate tax expense for the third quarter of 2026 was $12 million compared to $81 million for the third quarter of 2025. The decrease in corporate tax expense is the result of Genmab\u2019s lower net profit before tax and decrease in the estimated annual effective tax rate to 3.4% in the third quarter of 2026 compared to 18.9% in the third quarter of 2025. The lower estimated annual effective tax rate was primarily attributable to the Merus integration that allows for the partial recognition of previously unrecognized tax benefits. Accordingly, the estimated annual effective tax rate for the third quarter of 2026 is not indicative of the Company's expected annual effective tax rate in future periods. The annual effective tax rate may experience further volatility from the statutory rate as Merus integration continues. Net Profit\nNet profit for the first nine months of 2026 was $698 million compared to $932 million in the first nine months of 2025. The decrease was driven by the items described above. Net profit for the third quarter of 2026 was $342 million compared to $401 million in the third quarter of 2025. The decrease was driven by the items described above.\nLiquidity and Capital Resources\nSeptember 30, December 31,\n2026 2025\nCash and cash equivalents $ 1,860 $ 1,715\nShareholders' equity $ 6,333 $ 5,847\nNon-current borrowings $ 4,674 $ 5,001\nCurrent borrowings $ 318 $ 273\nNine Months Ended September 30,\n2026 2025 Change\nNet cash provided by operating activities $ 574 $ 885 $ (311)\nNet cash (used in) investing activities $ (16) $ (71) $ 55\nNet cash (used in) financing activities $ (403) $ (440) $ 37\nNet increase in cash and cash equivalents $ 155 $ 374 $ (219)\nExchange Rate adjustments $ (10) $ 7 $ (17)\n"}
{"page": 29, "text": "Net cash provided by operating activities is primarily related to our operating profit, changes in operating assets and liabilities, reversal of net financial items, adjustments related to non-cash transactions, interest received and paid and corporate taxes paid. Net cash provided by operating activities was $574 million for the first nine months of 2026. This was primarily driven by Genmab\u2019s operating profit generated during the period of $986 million, adjustments for non-cash transactions of $233 million, and interest received of $42 million. These increases were partly offset by $354 million of unfavorable changes in working capital, interest paid of $231 million, primarily related to borrowings, and taxes paid of $97 million. Net cash provided by operating activities was $885 million for the first nine months of 2025. This was primarily driven by Genmab\u2019s operating profit generated during the period of $1,007 million, adjustments for non-cash transactions of $143 million, and interest received of $84 million. These increases were partly offset by taxes paid of $317 million and unfavorable working capital changes of $28 million. The $311 million decrease in net cash provided by operating activities in the first nine months of 2026 compared to the first nine months of 2025 is primarily driven by higher working capital outflows and interest payments, partly offset by lower corporate taxes paid.\nGenmab A/S\nCarl Jacobsens Vej 30\n2500 Valby, Denmark Tel: +45 7020 2728 www.genmab.com Company Announcement no. 45 Page 23/45 CVR no. 2102 3884\nInterim Report for the Nine Months of 2026 Net cash used in investing activities primarily reflects differences between the proceeds received from the sale and maturity of our investments and amounts invested, and the cash paid for investments in tangible and intangible assets. The $55 million decrease in net cash used in investing activities was primarily driven by the absence of marketable securities activity during the first nine months of 2026 following the liquidation of all marketable securities to contribute to the funding of the Merus acquisition completed in December 2025, compared to net marketable securities purchased of $26 million in the first nine months of 2025, lower investments in intangible and tangible assets of $23 million, as well as distributions from our fund investments of $8 million during the first nine months of 2026 compared to the first nine months of 2025.\n"}
{"page": 30, "text": "Net cash used in financing activities is primarily related to the repayments of borrowings, purchase of treasury shares, exercise of warrants, lease payments, and payment of withholding taxes on behalf of employees on net settled Restricted Stock Units (RSUs). The $37 million decrease in net cash used in financing activities between the periods was primarily driven by $334 million of higher cash paid for the purchase of treasury shares during the first nine months of 2025 compared to the first nine months of 2026 due to the timing of share repurchases, partially offset by $288 million of cash principal repayments on borrowings during the first nine months of 2026 compared to none in the first nine months of 2025 as Genmab did not have any borrowings prior to December 2025.\nBalance Sheet\n"}
{"page": 31, "text": "As of September 30, 2026, total assets were $12,936 million compared to $12,873 million on December 31, 2025. The increase of $63 million was primarily driven by an increase in cash and cash equivalents, receivables and other current assets and right-of-use assets, partly offset by a decrease in other intangible assets. Cash and cash equivalents increased by $145 million due to the operating profit generated during the period, partly offset by working capital movements, repayments of principal and interest related to borrowings and corporate taxes paid. Receivables and other current assets increased $129 million due to higher DARZALEX and Kesimpta royalties achieved under our collaborations with J&J and Novartis, driven by higher net sales in the third quarter of 2026 as compared to the fourth quarter of 2025, as well as higher trade receivables resulting from increased EPKINLY sales during the same period. Right-of-use assets increased $58 million, primarily related to the commencement of a lease for a manufacturing facility in the U.S. These increases were partly offset by a decrease of $281 million related to other intangible assets due to negative foreign exchange rate impacts from the weakening of the EUR against the USD on our EUR denominated intangible assets. As of September 30, 2026, total liabilities were $6,603 million compared to $7,026 million on December 31, 2025. The decrease in total liabilities of $423 million was primarily driven by a decrease in current other payables, non-current and current borrowings and taxes payable, partly offset by an increase in non-current and current lease liabilities. The decrease in current other payables of $137 million, was primarily driven by higher accruals recorded at year-end 2025 related to accrued termination costs associated with the discontinuance of the acasunlimab and other programs during the fourth quarter of 2025, accrued compensation and bonuses and accrued withholding tax on Merus related option payments paid in the first nine months of 2026. Non-current and current borrowings decreased $282 million, primarily driven by principal repayments on term loans A and B, as well as a decrease in corporate tax payable of $43 million. These decreases were partly offset by an increase in non-current and current lease liabilities of $54 million related to the commencement of a lease for a manufacturing facility in the U.S. Shareholders\u2019 equity as of September 30, 2026, was $6,333 million compared to $5,847 million on December 31, 2025. The increase of $486 million, or 8%, was primarily driven by Genmab\u2019s net profit for the period and share-based compensation expenses, partly offset by negative foreign exchange rate impacts affecting the translation of our subsidiaries into USD, mainly the EUR based subsidiaries as a result of the weakening of the EUR against the USD and the purchase of treasury shares. Employees\n"}
